{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"laws of exponents laws of exponents","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md","fileName":"Laws of Exponents.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Laws of Exponents","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Exponents and Roots]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Zero Exponent","Negative Exponent","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"laws of exponents laws of exponents","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md","fileName":"Laws of Exponents.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Laws of Exponents","heading":"Laws of Exponents","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The laws of exponents summarize how repeated factors combine. For appropriate real values and integer exponents,\n\n$$a^ma^n=a^{m+n},$$\n\n$$\\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\\quad(a\\neq0),$$\n\n$$(a^m)^n=a^{mn},$$\n\n$$(ab)^n=a^nb^n.$$\n\nEach law follows by counting and regrouping factors. The common-base requirement matters: exponents cannot be added merely because two powers are multiplied. These rules also explain the [[Zero Exponent]] and [[Negative Exponent]] conventions and support simplification of polynomial and rational terms.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Zero Exponent","Negative Exponent","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Laws of Exponents.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":